// src/server/protocol-guide.ts
var GUIDE_SECTIONS = {
  setup: `# Setting Up a Qino-Protocol Workspace

A qino-protocol workspace is a directory of interconnected knowledge nodes \u2014 concepts, plans, tools, research \u2014 organized as a navigable graph.

## File Structure

A workspace contains:

\`\`\`
graph.json                    # Workspace index \u2014 lists edges between nodes
.claude/qino-config.json      # Workspace identity and configuration
nodes/                        # Node directories (or custom nodesDir)
  my-concept/
    node.json                 # Identity: title, type, status, tags
    story.md                  # The impulse \u2014 why this node exists
    content/                  # Domain-specific files (iterations, research, etc.)
    annotations/              # Agent signals (observations, proposals)
    data/                     # Structured data (JSON files)
\`\`\`

## Structural Vocabulary

| File | Role | Required |
|------|------|----------|
| graph.json | Workspace index. Contains id, title, edges array. Nodes are discovered from the filesystem, not listed here. | Yes |
| .claude/qino-config.json | Workspace identity. Contains name and optional configuration. | Yes |
| node.json | Node identity. Fields: title (required), type, status, tags, edges. | Per node |
| story.md | The impulse \u2014 one or two sentences capturing what this node IS. Not a summary; a seed. | Per node |
| content/ | Domain files. Iterations, research documents, concept material. Discovered by filename. | Optional |
| annotations/ | Marginal signals from agents. Numbered markdown files with YAML frontmatter. | Optional |
| data/ | Structured JSON data. Evaluation results, scores, schema definitions. | Optional |

## Workspace Archetypes

Different projects call for different structures. Templates seed the right node types, colors, and usage hints:

| Template | Purpose | Node types |
|----------|---------|------------|
| **research** | Open-ended inquiry | inquiry, finding, session, reference |
| **concepts** | Vision and design for products and ideas | app, tool, ecosystem, capture |
| **implementation** | Building software | app, package, tool, infra, reference |
| **evaluation** | Quality assessment | evaluation, comparison, simulation, snapshot |
| **general** | Flexible starting point | reference, exploration |

**Present these options to the user** when initializing a workspace. The template choice shapes the type vocabulary \u2014 what kinds of nodes the workspace expects.

## Initialization

Use \`init_workspace\` to create a new workspace. It creates exactly three things:
1. \`graph.json\` \u2014 empty graph ready for nodes
2. \`.claude/qino-config.json\` \u2014 workspace identity with types, colors, and hints
3. The nodes directory (default: \`nodes/\`)

**Before calling init_workspace**: Ask the user:
- What should the workspace be called? (becomes the graph ID \u2014 lowercase, hyphens, underscores)
- Which template fits? (describe the archetypes above)
- Where should nodes live? (default: \`nodes/\` \u2014 most workspaces use this)

## After Initialization

Once the workspace exists, refine its config and start creating nodes.

### Configure the Workspace

Use \`update_config\` to adjust workspace identity and conventions:

\`\`\`json
{
  "name": "My Research",
  "color": "cyan",
  "types": {
    "inquiry": { "color": "violet", "hint": "Open question or investigation thread" },
    "finding": { "color": "teal", "hint": "Settled insight \u2014 something we now know" },
    "session": { "color": "amber", "hint": "Time-bounded session with specific focus" }
  },
  "statuses": {
    "composted": { "treatment": "faded" }
  }
}
\`\`\`

**Configurable fields:**
| Field | Effect |
|-------|--------|
| \`name\` | Display name for the workspace |
| \`landingTitle\` | Title shown on the landing page (falls back to name) |
| \`color\` | Workspace identity color from the palette: blue, orange, emerald, red, violet, pink, cyan, amber, rose, teal, indigo, zinc. Controls page tint. |
| \`editor\` | Preferred editor \u2014 "cursor", "zed", or default "code" (VS Code) |
| \`types\` | Map of node type \u2192 { color, hint }. Each type has a color from the palette and a hint describing when to use it. Controls graph labels, minimap dots, and type badges. Types not in config render in neutral gray. |
| \`statuses\` | Map of status \u2192 { treatment }. \`treatment: "faded"\` reduces node opacity for composted/dormant nodes. |

**Types are the workspace vocabulary.** Each type's hint tells agents when to use it. Always call \`read_config\` before creating nodes to see declared types. When introducing a genuinely new type, add it via \`update_config\` first \u2014 include a hint so future agents know when to use it.

### Create Nodes

Use \`create_node\` to structure content into the graph. Use types from \`read_config\` for visual consistency.

Tips for restructuring existing content:
- Each distinct concept, project, or topic becomes a node
- The node's story.md captures the essence \u2014 not a table of contents
- Existing files can become content/ files within the appropriate node
- Connections between topics become edges with context sentences
- Start with 3-5 nodes and grow organically \u2014 don't try to graph everything at once

## Related Sections

- \`structure\` \u2014 Node anatomy, lifecycle, type vocabulary
- \`edges\` \u2014 Connecting nodes with meaningful relationships
- \`annotations\` \u2014 Adding marginal signals to nodes`,
  structure: `# Node Structure

## Node Anatomy

Each node lives in its own directory under the workspace's nodes directory.

### node.json \u2014 Identity

\`\`\`json
{
  "title": "Human-readable title",
  "type": "concept",
  "status": "active",
  "tags": ["optional", "tags"],
  "edges": [
    { "to": "other-node", "label": "short phrase", "context": "Why this connection matters" }
  ]
}
\`\`\`

Fields:
- **title** (required): Display name for the node
- **type** (optional): From workspace vocabulary \u2014 e.g., "concept", "app", "research", "tool"
- **status** (optional): Lifecycle state \u2014 typically "active" or "composted"
- **tags** (optional): Freeform labels for filtering
- **edges** (optional): Connections to other nodes (source is implicit \u2014 it's this node)

### story.md \u2014 The Impulse

One or two sentences capturing what this node IS. Not a summary of its contents \u2014 a seed that orients anyone arriving here. Write it as if answering: "Why does this node exist?"

### content/ \u2014 Domain Files

Any files relevant to this node. Naming convention: \`{NN}-{descriptive-name}.md\` for ordered iterations. No schema enforced \u2014 the workspace defines its own conventions.

### data/ \u2014 Structured Data

JSON files for evaluation results, scores, or other structured content. Optional \`schema.json\` describes the data shape.

## Node Lifecycle

- **active**: Living node, being worked on or referenced
- **composted**: No longer active but preserved in the graph. Hidden from attention lists but still navigable. Composting is not deletion \u2014 it's natural lifecycle.

## Open Schema

The workspace defines its own type vocabulary. There is no fixed set of types \u2014 use whatever makes sense for the domain. Common patterns:
- Research workspaces: "concept", "research", "finding", "question"
- Implementation workspaces: "app", "tool", "ecosystem", "infrastructure"
- Mixed workspaces: whatever emerges from the content

Check \`read_config\` for the workspace's declared types and statuses. Use \`update_config\` to add new types with colors when introducing a new type to the workspace.

## Visual Configuration

Type and status styling flows from the workspace config (\`.claude/qino-config.json\`):

- **Workspace color**: Top-level \`color\` field sets the page tint for workspace identity.
- **Type \u2192 color**: Each type has a named color from the 12-color palette (blue, orange, emerald, red, violet, pink, cyan, amber, rose, teal, indigo, zinc). Controls type labels, graph minimap dots, and badges.
- **Status \u2192 treatment**: Statuses can have a \`treatment\` value. Currently supported: \`"faded"\` (reduces node opacity for composted or dormant nodes).

Types without a declared color render in neutral gray. When creating nodes with a type not in config, the tool will suggest adding it via \`update_config\`.

## Node Placement

The root graph (workspace root) is for **orientation** \u2014 navigators, arcs. These are consumed from the landing page, top-down.

Workspace graphs are for **participation** \u2014 apps, tools, concepts, research. These need bidirectional edges and lateral discovery through neighborhoods.

**Rule:** If a node needs to be discovered from other nodes' neighborhoods (via edges and connectedSignals), put it in the workspace graph where its consumers live. Root graph nodes can point into workspaces, but workspace nodes cannot point back to the root.`,
  edges: `# Edges \u2014 Connecting Nodes

## Edge Fields

Edges connect nodes with semantic meaning. They live in the source node's \`node.json\` and are assembled into \`graph.json\` by the protocol.

\`\`\`json
{
  "to": "target-node-id",
  "label": "short phrase",
  "context": "A sentence explaining why this connection exists and what it means.",
  "weight": 2
}
\`\`\`

| Field | Role | Example |
|-------|------|---------|
| to | Target node ID | "ecosystem-design" |
| label | 2-4 word insight this edge carries | "coherence frontier" |
| context | Sentence explaining the connection | "Calibration revealed coherence as the next architectural frontier" |
| weight | Visual priority: 1 quiet, 2 normal (default), 3 prominent | 2 |

## Context Is the Primary Carrier

The context sentence is what agents read when navigating the graph. It should answer: "Why does this connection matter?" A good context sentence is specific \u2014 it captures the insight, not just the fact that two nodes are related.

Good: "Walk's relational grammar informed how World structures figure encounters"
Weak: "These two are related"

## Cross-Graph Edges

To connect nodes across different workspaces, use the \`graphPath:nodeId\` format for the target:

\`\`\`json
{ "to": "qino-concepts:ecosystem-design", "context": "Design principles originated in the concepts workspace" }
\`\`\`

The graphPath matches the workspace path from \`read_activity\`.

## Connection Annotations

When you notice a connection while reading a node, use \`write_annotation\` with signal "connection". This creates an annotation that can later be promoted to a proper edge with \`add_edge\`.

## Creating Edges

Two tools for creating edges:
- \`create_node\` \u2014 include edges when creating a new node (most common)
- \`add_edge\` \u2014 connect two existing nodes after the fact

Only create edges that earn their place. Each should carry a meaningful insight. Read the surrounding graph to match the label register \u2014 research edges carry discoveries, concept edges carry structural relationships.`,
  annotations: `# Annotations \u2014 Marginal Signals

## Four Signal Types

Ordered by intensity \u2014 from quiet observation to active proposal:

| Signal | When to use | Example |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| **reading** | Observation made while reading. No action needed. | "The pattern here echoes what emerged in the walk research" |
| **connection** | Noticed link to another node or concept. May become an edge. | "This connects to the calibration methodology \u2014 same convergence pattern" |
| **tension** | Something doesn't sit right. Needs human attention. | "The stated scope conflicts with what iteration 3 actually built" |
| **proposal** | Concrete suggestion for change or development. | "Consider splitting this node \u2014 the research findings and the design decisions serve different audiences" |

## File Format

Annotations are numbered markdown files in the node's \`annotations/\` directory:

\`\`\`
annotations/
  001-pattern-observation.md
  002-scope-tension.md
  003-split-proposal.md
\`\`\`

Each file has YAML frontmatter:

\`\`\`markdown
---
signal: proposal
created: 2026-03-15
target: content/03-iteration.md
status: open
---

The iteration scope has grown beyond what a single node can hold.
Consider splitting research findings into their own node.
\`\`\`

## Annotation Lifecycle

1. **open** (default) \u2014 Created, waiting for attention
2. **accepted** \u2014 Human approves; agent should act on it
3. **resolved** \u2014 Addressed; no more attention needed
4. **dismissed** \u2014 Not relevant; hidden from attention lists

Use \`write_annotation\` to create annotations. Use \`resolve_annotation\` to update lifecycle status. Resolved and dismissed annotations are filtered out of action items in \`read_graph\`.

## When to Annotate vs. When to Create a Node

- **Annotate** when the thought is *about* an existing node \u2014 a marginal signal, not standalone content
- **Create a node** when the thought deserves its own identity \u2014 it will be referenced, extended, or composed with other nodes
- **Neither** for ephemeral working notes that don't need to persist`
};

